# converted from 10-the-cup.md
@title The Cup
@bg mire
@mood grief

narrate He left before dawn.
narrate He did it properly, because he did everything properly, because a man who has decided not to feel anything still has to decide how to wash a cup.
narrate He rinsed it in the stream, twice, the way he had rinsed it every morning for eleven weeks. He dried it on his sleeve. And then he set it down on a flat stone at the edge of the camp, upside down, the way you leave a cup when you have finished and are not coming back.
narrate The dog watched him do it. The dog watched him the whole time, from the other side of the fire, with an expression of enormous and specific judgement, and did not bark, because the dog had understood eleven weeks ago what Cael was and had decided, on balance, to allow it.
cael neutral You'll look after him,
narrate The dog did not dignify this with a response.
narrate Here is what he did not do: he did not wake the boy.
narrate He had considered it. He had lain awake for four hours considering it, and he had written three speeches in his head, and all three of them were good, and all three of them were lies.
narrate The first speech was the useful one. You will take the mark, and here is exactly what to say to it, and here is how to word the request so that it takes the Fever and not you, and here is what it will cost and here is how to survive the cost. That was a true speech and it was a good one and he did not give it, because the boy did not need it, because the boy had already worked out the wording on his own in a field four days ago and had said it out loud to the grass and had got it right the first time.
narrate The second speech was the honest one. I have been arranging you for eleven weeks. I made you tea to see what you would do with it. I let the Hollow burn partly because I wanted you to watch. You are the only person I have spoken to in twelve years who has not been a tool, and I have used you anyway, and I am sorry.
narrate That was true and he did not give it either, because an apology from a man who is leaving is not an apology. It is a receipt.
narrate The third speech was the one he actually wanted to give, and it had no words in it at all. It was: stay. It was: don't do it, I'll find another way, there is always another way, I have been telling myself that for twelve years and it is comfortable and I want you to be comfortable. It was: I don't want to be the only one who knows what this costs.
narrate He did not give that one either, because it was the voice of the thing that had stood in the reeds outside his own burning house and decided not to feel anything.
narrate He had spent twelve years being that thing. He was not going to let it have the last word.
narrate So he washed the cup, and turned it upside down, and left.
narrate He walked north for six hours.
narrate And somewhere around the fourth, with the Mire grey on every side and the dog's absence a specific weight in the air behind him, Cael worked out the thing that he had been failing to work out for twelve years.
narrate He had never needed to be a monster.
narrate That was it. That was the whole of it. He had spent twelve years building the Coven, and burning the Hollow, and standing in the trees at Sallow's Rest doing the arithmetic while forty-one people died — and he had told himself, every day, that it was necessary. That it was penance. That he had made a selfish choice four years after Ilsa died, and that the only way to balance the ledger was to become something that could not make selfish choices anymore.
narrate But that was not penance. Penance is what you do to make something right. What Cael had done was what you do to make sure you never have to try again.
narrate He had not been punishing himself. He had been protecting himself — building a man so hard and so empty that nobody could ever again hand him a child and ask him to choose.
narrate And it had worked. He had succeeded. He had built exactly the thing he set out to build.
narrate And the first person it had failed to protect him from was a nineteen-year-old soldier who said there's no milk in a burned kitchen.
narrate He stopped walking.
narrate He stood in the middle of the Mire, four hours north of a boy who was going to stand at a hole in a field and spend everything, and he understood — with the particular clarity of a man who has spent twelve years being certain — that he was about to be forgiven.
narrate He did not want it. That was the terrible thing. He stood in the grey and looked at the place where the forgiveness was going to come from, and every part of him that had survived twelve years wanted to turn around and go back and stop it, because a man who has built himself out of guilt does not want to be forgiven; he wants to be right.
narrate And he stood there for a long time.
narrate And then he did the arithmetic one more time — the last time, as it turned out, the very last time Cael ever did the arithmetic — and it came out the way it had come out in the trees, and in the reeds, and on the grass at the edge of a hole in a field.
narrate Maeve had said that to him. Twelve years ago, on the floor beside a chair.
narrate He had never once believed it. He believed it now.
narrate He walked north.
narrate He had five years left and a mark on his palm and a daughter he could still almost hear, and he was going to spend all of it getting to the one place in the world where he might see the boy again.
narrate He would see him again. He did not know that yet — he was simply walking, because walking was what he did, and because the alternative was standing still.
narrate But he would. In a year, in the ruins of a place that had not been built yet, with a broken mark and a name he could not say, Cael would see Corin again.
narrate And the thing he would say — the only thing, in the end, that he had left to give — he had already said.
